    "exit": 0,
    "stdout": "---\nname: grit\ndescription: Use the grit CLI for version control in Git repositories — status, diffs, commits, branches, merges, cherry-picks, reverts, fetch/pull/push and tags — with JSON output for scripts and agents. Use when a task involves committing, branching, syncing with a remote or reading history and `grit` is installed.\n---\n\n# grit\n\n`grit` is a small, opinionated Git client. It reads and writes ordinary Git repositories, so `git` and `grit` can be used on the same repository. It does not mirror Git's commands or flags: there is usually one obvious way to do each thing, and every command can answer in JSON.\n\nThis skill was generated by `grit [VERSION]`. Run `grit <command> --help` for the exact options of the installed version.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Pass `--json` whenever you will read the result. stdout then holds exactly one JSON object; progress and prompts go to stderr.\n- Use `--filter '<jq-like expr>'` (with `--json`) to keep only what you need: `grit status --json --filter '{branch, clean}'`.\n- On failure the exit code is 1 and stdout is `{\"error\": \"…\"}`. Exit code 2 is a usage error (unknown flag or command) and has no JSON.\n- Always give `grit commit` a message. It never opens an editor.\n- Don't translate Git habits flag-for-flag. Git's `git add -p`, `rebase`, and `reset` have no grit equivalent; use `grit add --dry-run`, path-scoped `grit add`, and `grit commit --staged` / `-- <paths>` instead of inventing `-a` or `-p` flags (see \"What grit doesn't do\").\n\n## Where am I?\n\n```console\n$ grit status --json\n```\n\nPlain `grit` is the same as `grit status`. Keys: `branch`, `detached`, `head`, `target` (trunk your work is headed for), `ahead`, `behind` (vs `target`; `null` with `--no-ahead-behind` or `status.aheadBehind=false`), `upstream` (`{ref, ahead, behind}` when tracking differs from `target`, else null), `commits` (up to ten ahead of the target; each row includes `change_id` and `change_id_stored`), `staged`, `unstaged`, `untracked`, `clean`, `stash_count`. Pass `--ignored` to add an `ignored` path list (same set as Git's `!!` porcelain lines).\n\nLimit to paths with `grit status -- <path>…` (same pathspec rules as `grit add`). Untracked listing: `--untracked no|normal|all`. `--short` prints one line per path for humans (two-letter index/worktree codes; `--json` is unchanged). Staged renames in `--json` use `{ \"status\": \"renamed\", \"path\": \"new\", \"old_path\": \"old\" }`; human output shows `old → new`. Rename detection follows `status.renames` / `diff.renames` (on by default; set `status.renames=false` to disable).\n\nThe target branch is the first that exists of: the `target.branch` config value, `origin/master`, `origin/main`, `master`, `main`. Change it with `grit config target.branch origin/develop`.\n\n## Reading history and changes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Uncommitted changes as a diff | `grit diff` |\n| Changed paths only | `grit diff --name-only` |\n| Per-file line counts | `grit diff --stat` or `grit diff --numstat` |\n| The change one commit made | `grit show <commit>` (patch by default; merge commits use combined diffs unless `--diff-merges=first-parent`) |\n| Several commits at once | `grit show <A> <B>` (`--json` → `{ \"objects\": [ … ] }`) |\n| Blob or tree at a revision | `grit show <rev>:<path>` (same resolution as `grit cat`) |\n| A commit's message and diffstat only | `grit show [<commit-or-branch-or-tag>] --stat` |\n| Recent history, ten at a time | `grit log`, then `grit log --before <next>` using the `next` value |\n| History with patches or file lists | `grit log -p`, `grit log --stat`, `grit log --name-status`, … (same format flags as `grit diff`) |\n| Commits on this branch not on the target | `grit shortlog` |\n\nRevisions can be full or short object ids, branch or tag names, change ids (reverse-hex `k`–`z`, 4–32 chars — unique prefix or full id), or expressions like `HEAD~2` and `\\<cid\\>~1`.\n\nCommit rows in `--json` from `log`, `show`, `revs`, and `status` share `change_id` (reverse-hex) and `change_id_stored`. Human `log` and `show` prefix each commit with `change <8 chars>`; `--oneline` does not.\n\n## Committing\n\n```console\n$ grit commit -m \"Explain what changed and why\"\n```\n\n`grit commit` **auto-stages** the whole worktree by default (`commit.autoStage=true`). Use **`grit commit --staged`** to commit only the index (set `commit.autoStage=false` to make that the default). Use **`grit commit -m msg -- <paths>`** (or `--pathspec-from-file`) for a partial commit that leaves other staged changes in the index — like `git commit --only`. **`--dry-run`** previews the commit (`dry_run`, `files`, and related fields in `--json`) without writing objects or moving refs. **`-F -`** reads the message from stdin (same as `-m`, but non-interactive). `-a` / `--all` forces auto-stage; `--staged`, paths, and `-a` are mutually exclusive.\n\nTypical agent loop on a clone with `origin` set:\n\n1. `grit status --json --filter '{branch, staged, unstaged, untracked, clean}' -- <dir>` — scope to the files you care about (`--json` must come before `--`).\n2. `grit add --dry-run -u -- <paths>` then `grit add -u -- <paths>` (or `grit add -- <paths>` for explicit files).\n3. `grit commit --dry-run -m \"…\"` (or `--staged` / path scope) before the real commit.\n4. `grit commit --staged -F -` or `grit commit -m \"…\"` when ready.\n\nAfter each step you can cross-check with `git status --porcelain=v2`, `git fsck --strict`, and `git log`.\n\n### Staging (`grit add`)\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stage everything (modified, deleted, untracked) | `grit add` |\n| Stage only tracked edits and deletions | `grit add -u` (or `grit add --update`) |\n| Preview without writing the index | `grit add --dry-run` (combine with `-u` or paths) |\n| Stage specific paths | `grit add -- path/to/file` |\n\n`grit add --json` returns `{added, modified, removed, dry_run, count, staged}` with repository-relative paths. Use `--dry-run` before bulk staging to bound agent work; path lists are sorted and stable.\n\n`grit commit` fails with \"nothing to commit\" on a clean tree unless you pass `--allow-empty`. It also refuses a detached HEAD.\n\nEach commit gets a stable **`change-id`** header (on by default) that survives `--amend`. Disable with `grit config grit.changeIds false` when you need Git-identical commit objects. See the docs site section on change ids for derivation and testing config.\n\n## Branches\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| List branches | `grit branch` |\n| Create a branch at the current commit (no switch) | `grit branch <name>` |\n| Create and switch | `grit switch -c <name>` |\n| Switch | `grit switch <name>` (also `grit checkout`, `grit co`) |\n| Delete a merged branch | `grit branch -d <name>` |\n| Delete regardless | `grit branch -D <name>` |\n\n`grit switch`, `grit merge`, `grit pick`, `grit revert` and `grit pull` refuse to run with uncommitted changes. Commit first. They also refuse rather than overwrite an untracked file.\n\n## Combining work\n\n- `grit merge <branch>` fast-forwards when it can, otherwise records a merge commit. `<branch>` can be local or remote-tracking (`origin/main`).\n- `grit pick <commit>` applies one non-merge commit to the current branch as a new commit, keeping its author and message.\n- `grit revert <commit>` undoes one non-merge commit with a new inverse commit (`Revert \"…\"` message).\n- On a conflict, `grit merge` / `grit pick` / `grit revert` exit 1 and leave conflict markers. Run **`grit status --json`** and read **`conflicts`** (typed entries with `path` and `kind`) and **`operation.next_steps`** (ordered grit commands; use `shell`-style quoting when a step includes a message). Fix files, stage resolved paths with `grit add`, then run the suggested `grit commit`. **`operation.alternatives`** lists actions such as `grit merge --abort`.\n\n## Remotes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Copy a repository | `grit clone <url> [<dir>]` |\n| List or add remotes | `grit remote`, `grit remote add <name> <url>` |\n| Download without changing your branch | `grit fetch [<remote>]` |\n| Fetch and merge the remote copy of this branch | `grit pull` |\n| Publish this branch to `origin` under the same name | `grit push` |\n| Publish all local tags | `grit push --tags` |\n\n`grit push` never force-pushes. If the remote has commits you don't, the push is rejected (exit 1, `\"rejected\": true`); run `grit pull`, then push again.\n\nURLs can be `https://`, `ssh://`, `user@host:path`, `git://`, `file://` or a local path.\n\n## Bundles and linked worktrees\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Offline pack transfer | `grit bundle create`, `verify`, `list`, `unbundle` |\n| Extra working trees | `grit worktree list`, `add`, `remove`, `lock`, `unlock`, `prune` |\n\n## Tags and config\n\n- `grit tag` lists tags, `grit tag <name>` creates a lightweight tag at the current commit, `grit tag -d <name>` deletes one.\n- `grit config <key>` reads, `grit config <key> <value>` sets, `grit config --unset <key>` removes, `grit config --list` lists. Add `--global` for the per-user file.\n\n## Staying non-interactive\n\n- HTTPS GitHub: run `grit auth` once in an interactive session (it uses a browser device flow). After that, fetch, pull and push use the stored token. When stdin isn't a terminal, grit won't prompt to sign in; it fails with a hint instead.\n- SSH: set `GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes'` so a missing key or unknown host fails fast instead of waiting for input.\n- Don't run `grit update` in automation; it reinstalls the binary.\n- `grit manager`, `grit upload-pack` and `grit receive-pack` are plumbing that speaks Git protocols on stdin/stdout. Don't call them directly.\n\n## What grit doesn't do\n\ngrit has no equivalent of `rebase`, `reset`, interactive patch selection (`git add -p`), or annotated tags. Use `grit add`, `grit commit --staged`, and path arguments for precise staging; use `grit commit --amend` (optional message, JSON `amended` field) to rewrite the tip commit. For `grit stash pop` or `grit stash apply` with conflicts, finish with `git` if grit leaves the entry in place. When a task needs something on this list, use `git` on the same repository and return to `grit` afterwards.\n\n## More\n\n- Docs for every command, with JSON field tables: https://grit-scm.com/docs/\n- Agent guide (output contract, exit codes, credentials): https://grit-scm.com/docs/agents/\n- All docs as one text file: https://grit-scm.com/llms-full.txt\n",
